/* Pass 25 what-the-armor-leaves-behind; publishes existing Pass 24 chapters 14,32,48 and new 17,42. */
(function(){'use strict';var lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;var edits={"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-14":{"number":14,"title":"Cutler","subtitle":"What the Armor Leaves Behind · Chapter 14 of 40","content":"Danse did not sleep after Fort Strong.\n\nI knew because I did not sleep either.\n\nThe Prydwen’s night cycle dimmed the lights but never silenced the ship. Engines thrummed through the deck. Pipes clicked. Boots passed in corridors. The Brotherhood could imitate darkness; it could not imitate rest.\n\nI found Danse in the maintenance bay at 0300 cleaning a weapon that was already clean.\n\nAt the other end of the bay, Haylen was checking the casualty supply ledger. She looked up when Danse set the rifle on the table for the third time.\n\n“Paladin, I need you to confirm two parts requests before the morning shift,” she said.\n\nHe reached for the ledger without looking at her. She kept her hand on it.\n\n“Read the quantities. Don't just sign.”\n\nDanse glanced up, irritated. I could see the argument ready on his tongue. Then he saw the two handwritten amendments in the margin.\n\n“Three filters?” he asked. “We only logged two damaged.”\n\n“The third came from the field kit you used at Fort Strong. It isn't on the return sheet.”\n\nHe stared at the page. The station breathed around us: engine vibration, the ticking of a cooling pipe, someone dragging a crate across the next deck.\n\n“Put it against my allocation,” he said.\n\n“It goes against the mission inventory,” Haylen replied. “You don't personally owe the Brotherhood every piece of equipment that wears out.”\n\nIt was such a small sentence. Danse looked as if she had taken away a rule he'd relied on for years.\n\nHaylen gave me a brief nod and went back to the radio desk. There was nothing tender about the exchange. She was exhausted, and she needed the records to be right. I admired her for insisting.\n\nDanse signed the correct line. When he finally put down his pen, there was a tremor at the base of his thumb.\n\n“You don't have to tell me about Cutler tonight,” I said.\n\n“I was not planning to.”\n\n“Okay.”\n\nHe took another breath, then one more. “The way Haylen said that—about the filter. Cutler used to argue with me about missing kit. He said I'd inventory the whole world if they gave me a large enough clipboard.”\n\nI smiled before I could help it. “Was he wrong?”\n\nDanse's mouth moved. Not quite a smile. “Not entirely.”\n\nHe told me no more that night. I sat on the workbench until the maintenance bay's lights changed for morning, close enough for him to know I hadn't gone, far enough to let the memory stay his.\n\n“You missed a spot,” I said.\n\nHe did not look up.\n\n“No, I didn’t.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nI sat on the bench opposite him.\n\nHe continued running cloth through the rifle barrel.\n\n“You don’t have to stay.”\n\n“Neither do you.”\n\n“I’m working.”\n\n“You’re polishing your emotions.”\n\nHis hands stopped.\n\n“That doesn’t make sense.”\n\n“It made sense in my head.”\n\n“That’s concerning.”\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe did not, but the tension eased.\n\nFor several minutes I watched him work.\n\nFinally he set the rifle down.\n\n“You want to know about Cutler.”\n\n“I want you to tell me whatever keeps you from dismantling that gun into atoms.”\n\nDanse leaned back.\n\n“The first time I met him, he tried to sell me my own scrap.”\n\nI blinked.\n\n“What?”\n\n“In Rivet City. I had a box of servo parts beside my stand. Walked away for maybe two minutes. When I came back, Cutler was trying to sell them to a customer.”\n\n“That’s incredible.”\n\n“I nearly broke his nose.”\n\n“Also incredible.”\n\n“He said if I left valuable inventory unattended, I deserved to lose it.”\n\n“What happened?”\n\n“I made him give me half the caps.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nDanse smiled down at his hands.\n\n“We became partners three days later.”\n\nThe story came slowly after that.\n\nRivet City markets. Junk runs through dangerous buildings. Splitting food when one of them had a bad week. Competing over who could find the strangest pre-War object and convincing tourists it had value.\n\n“Cutler once sold a broken hand dryer as a ‘portable wind generator.’”\n\n“Did it work?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Did the customer know?”\n\n“Eventually.”\n\n“Your Brotherhood origin story involves fraud.”\n\n“Minor misrepresentation.”\n\nI laughed so hard someone in the next bay told us to shut up.\n\nDanse looked younger when he spoke about those years.\n\nThen the story reached recruitment.\n\n“The Brotherhood came through Rivet City looking for people who could handle themselves. Cutler heard ‘regular meals’ and decided immediately.”\n\n“And you?”\n\n“I heard ‘training.’”\n\n“Of course you did.”\n\n“We thought we had nothing to lose.”\n\nHis smile faded.\n\n“That’s usually when people discover how much they had.”\n\nThe maintenance bay seemed colder.\n\nDanse stared at the rifle on the table.\n\n“Years later, when I found him in that nest, he looked at me.”\n\nI did not move.\n\n“His face was changing. His voice…” Danse swallowed. “I don’t know if he knew me.”\n\n“You said he didn’t attack.”\n\n“He didn’t.”\n\n“Did he speak?”\n\n“Not clearly.”\n\nDanse rubbed both hands over his face.\n\n“I fired before he finished changing.”\n\nThe sentence came out broken around the edges.\n\nI moved to the bench beside him.\n\nNot touching.\n\nClose.\n\n“I told myself it was mercy.”\n\n“Maybe it was.”\n\n“I told myself there was no person left to save.”\n\n“Maybe there wasn’t.”\n\nHe looked at me, frustrated.\n\n“You keep saying maybe.”\n\n“Because certainty would be a lie.”\n\nHis anger collapsed.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe looked down.\n\n“That’s what I hate.”\n\n“The not knowing?”\n\n“The possibility that I could be wrong and still have done something irreversible.”\n\nThere it was.\n\nNot hatred of mutants.\n\nFear of being the person who obeyed the rule and killed his friend anyway.\n\nI reached for his hand.\n\nThis time I did it slowly enough that he could move away.\n\nHe did not.\n\nMy fingers closed around his.\n\nDanse stared at them.\n\n“I don’t know what Cutler was in those last minutes,” I said. “But I know what he was before. Your friend. The person who knew you before the armor. Remembering that doesn’t betray the Brotherhood.”\n\nHis thumb shifted against mine.\n\n“No.”\n\n“And questioning what happened doesn’t either.”\n\n“That part is debatable.”\n\n“Then debate me.”\n\nHis mouth twisted.\n\n“At three in the morning?”\n\n“Coward.”\n\nHe gave me a tired, real smile.\n\nOur hands were still joined.\n\nNeither of us acknowledged it.\n\nEventually Danse said, “He would have made fun of me.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“This.”\n\nHe lifted our hands slightly.\n\nMy heart tripped.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I’m terrible at this.”\n\n“At holding hands?”\n\n“At…”\n\nHe gestured vaguely between us with his free hand.\n\n“This.”\n\nI decided mercy was appropriate.\n\n“Talking?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Terrible.”\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“You’re welcome.”\n\nI started to pull away.\n\nHis fingers tightened.\n\nJust for a second.\n\nThen he released me.\n\nThe absence of contact felt louder than it should have.\n\nDanse picked up the rifle again.\n\nHe did not resume cleaning it.\n\n“I haven’t told anyone that much about him.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“How?”\n\n“Because you looked like it hurt.”\n\nHe stared at me.\n\nThen nodded once.\n\n“Get some sleep.”\n\n“That sounds like an order.”\n\n“It is.”\n\n“You coming?”\n\nThe words left my mouth before I considered them.\n\nDanse’s eyes widened.\n\nI felt heat climb my face.\n\n“To the sleeping quarters,” I added quickly. “To sleep. Separately.”\n\n“Yes.” His voice cracked slightly. He cleared his throat. “Obviously.”\n\nWe walked back together without speaking.\n\nAt my bunk, he paused.\n\n“Good night.”\n\n“Good night, Danse.”\n\nHe started away.\n\nI lay down beneath the pipes.\n\nTen seconds later, from the corridor, I heard him mutter, “Outstanding.”\n\nI buried my face in the blanket so no one would hear me laugh.\n\nThe next morning I found him in the Prydwen’s mess with coffee so black it looked structurally unsound.\n\nHe had told me about Cutler during the night: Rivet City, the Brotherhood, the patrol, the super mutants, the thing he had been forced to do when the man he loved like a brother was no longer entirely the man he remembered.\n\nNow Danse stared into the cup like the answer might be at the bottom.\n\nI sat across from him.\n\n“Did you sleep?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Liar.”\n\n“Approximately forty minutes.”\n\n“That is not sleep. That is losing an argument with consciousness.”\n\nHe made a sound that might have been amusement.\n\nI took the coffee from his hand and tasted it.\n\nMy entire mouth objected.\n\n“What is wrong with you?”\n\nDanse looked genuinely confused.\n\n“It’s coffee.”\n\n“It’s a chemical weapon.”\n\n“It’s efficient.”\n\n“Food should not be evaluated by tactical efficiency.”\n\n“Coffee is not food.”\n\n“That is the first sensible thing you’ve said about it.”\n\nHe took the cup back.\n\nThe absurdity helped.\n\nFor a while.\n\nThen he said, “I should have found them sooner.”\n\nThere it was.\n\n“You told me the patrol disappeared in an irradiated area full of super mutants.”\n\n“I should have anticipated the route.”\n\n“You weren’t even commanding the mission.”\n\n“I was his friend.”\n\nThe sentence cracked in the middle.\n\nDanse looked away quickly.\n\nI did not tell him it was not his fault.\n\nHe would have rejected that because grief is not a courtroom and guilt does not care about evidence.\n\nInstead I asked, “What would Cutler say if he heard you taking responsibility for the Commonwealth existing around him?”\n\nDanse’s mouth tightened.\n\n“He’d tell me to stop being an idiot.”\n\n“Sounds wise.”\n\n“He was not.”\n\nThe smile came anyway.\n\n“What was he like before?”\n\nDanse leaned back.\n\n“Reckless.”\n\n“Compared to you, a chair is reckless.”\n\n“He cheated at target competitions.”\n\n“How?”\n\n“He’d adjust the sights on my rifle when I wasn’t looking.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nDanse did too.\n\nThen his eyes turned wet.\n\nHe looked furious about it.\n\n“I remember the last time I saw him as himself more clearly than I remember some entire years of my life.”\n\nI reached across the table.\n\nMy hand stopped halfway.\n\nChoice.\n\nDanse looked at it.\n\nThen placed his hand in mine.\n\nHis grip was careful at first.\n\nThen not.\n\n“I’m afraid,” he said quietly, “that if I let myself care about people the way I cared about him, I’ll lose them the same way.”\n\n“That’s possible.”\n\nHe looked down at our hands as if he had only just realized he was still holding mine.\n\n“Cutler used to say I could turn anything into an assignment,” he said.\n\n“Was he wrong?”\n\n“No.” A faint smile. “He once ordered me to take an afternoon off.”\n\n“That is deeply funny.”\n\n“I told him he lacked authority.”\n\n“What did he do?”\n\n“Stole my boots.”\n\nI laughed so suddenly a nearby knight glanced over.\n\nDanse’s smile widened before grief pulled at it.\n\n“I found them hanging from a pipe in Rivet City.”\n\nThe memory hurt him.\n\nIt also warmed him.\n\nFor once I did not try to separate those things.\n\nHe flinched at the answer.\n\nI held on.\n\n“I’m not going to promise the Commonwealth can’t take people. It can. You know that better than I do.”\n\n“Then why—”\n\n“Because not caring doesn’t save them.”\n\nHis gaze dropped to our joined hands.\n\n“It only makes you lose them early.”\n\nDanse went very still.\n\nAround us, soldiers ate breakfast, complained about duty shifts, and argued about ammunition requisitions. Life continued with rude efficiency.\n\nFinally his thumb moved across my knuckles.\n\n“You have an irritating habit of making arguments I can’t easily dismiss.”\n\n“You could try bad coffee.”\n\n“That is not an argument.”\n\n“Neither is this beverage.”\n\nThe laugh that came out of him was quiet and tired and real.\n\nHe did not release my hand until his coffee had gone cold.\n","word_count":1918},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-17":{"number":17,"title":"Benchwork","subtitle":"What the Armor Leaves Behind · Chapter 17 of 40","content":"Danse flirted like a man attempting to repair a radio with a combat knife.\n\nThat is to say: earnestly, dangerously, and without recognizing what he was doing.\n\nWe were at a weapon bench on the Prydwen when it started.\n\nI had Righteous Authority disassembled for another adjustment. Danse stood beside me sorting components.\n\n“You’ve improved,” he said.\n\n“At weapon modification?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“You no longer install parts backward.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“There it is.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“You almost complimented me without insult.”\n\n“That wasn’t an insult. It was an observation.”\n\n“Your romance technique must be devastating.”\n\nThe component in his hand stopped moving.\n\nI realized what I had said.\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“No. You said something about…”\n\n“I was joking.”\n\nHis ears began to turn red.\n\n“Right.”\n\nI returned my attention to the rifle with the concentration of someone defusing a bomb.\n\nDanse did the same.\n\nFor approximately thirty seconds.\n\nThen he said, “For the record, I’m not attempting to romance you.”\n\nI nearly dropped the barrel.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“Obviously.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nSilence.\n\nA scribe at the next bench quietly gathered her tools and left.\n\nCoward.\n\nDanse cleared his throat.\n\n“I only meant that your technical skills have improved.”\n\n“Understood.”\n\n“And I enjoy working with you.”\n\nMy hands stopped.\n\nHe seemed not to notice the second sentence was far more dangerous than the first.\n\n“I enjoy working with you too.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nAnother silence.\n\nHe picked up the wrong screw.\n\nI stared.\n\nDanse looked down, realized it, and set it back.\n\n“Don’t,” he said.\n\n“I did not say a word.”\n\n“You were going to.”\n\n“I notice patterns.”\n\nHe gave me a look that should have melted armor.\n\nInstead it made warmth spread through my chest.\n\nWe were on our way out of the maintenance bay when Haylen intercepted us with a clipboard, a crate, and the expression of somebody who had been told to ignore an obvious problem.\n\"The replacement reflex sight came without a calibration tag,\" she said. \"Supply wants us to sign it into service anyway.\"\n\"Probably works,\" the quartermaster called from his desk.\nDanse set his kit down, verified the rifle was clear, and examined the mounting points without touching the trigger. \"Probably is not a certification standard.\"\nHaylen read off two serial numbers. The shipment number belonged to another crate; the part itself had no record of having been tested.\n\"Two discrepancies,\" she said.\n\"At least two,\" Danse answered, \"and I will not create a third by signing a false inspection.\"\nThe quartermaster muttered something about being short of parts. Danse wrote SHORTAGE on the release form, signed a hold order, and returned the sight to its box.\nI watched him cap his pen. \"You know, a man who takes paperwork that seriously is dangerously attractive.\"\nHaylen made a sound that might have been a cough.\n\"Procedure is not a romantic gesture,\" Danse said.\n\"That's devastating news. Here I was, about to swoon over serial-number verification.\"\nHe gave me a look of strict disapproval, ruined by the slightest movement at the corner of his mouth. Haylen took the clipboard away before either of us could say something worse.\nThe other soldiers would never know we had delayed our evening to keep an untested part out of their hands. I liked that he had done it anyway. I liked even more that he had listened when Haylen brought him evidence, rather than treating rank as a substitute for being right.\n\nThat evening we took two beers to the observation deck.\n\nDanse had called it “decompression after duty.”\n\nI called it having a drink together.\n\nHe refused to call it anything.\n\nThe Commonwealth below was scattered with lights: Diamond City, settlements, campfires, places where people had decided darkness did not get everything.\n\nDanse leaned on the rail.\n\n“You ever think about leaving?” I asked.\n\n“The Brotherhood?”\n\n“The Commonwealth.”\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n“Where would I go?”\n\n“Anywhere.”\n\n“That’s not a destination.”\n\n“Neither is purpose.”\n\nHe took a drink.\n\n“You really dislike that word.”\n\n“I dislike when people use it instead of wanting something.”\n\n“Wanting isn’t always useful.”\n\n“Neither is beer.”\n\n“That’s debatable.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nDanse’s smile stayed after mine faded.\n\n“What?” I asked.\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“You were looking at me.”\n\n“I do that sometimes.”\n\nMy own words, returned.\n\nThe air between us changed.\n\nNeither of us looked away.\n\nDanse’s face was different when he was not actively trying to be a paladin. Softer around the eyes. Younger. There was a small scar near one eyebrow I had never asked about. His mouth was more expressive than he seemed to realize.\n\nI became extremely aware of it.\n\nHe looked at mine too.\n\nThen a vertibird roared past the deck.\n\nThe moment broke.\n\nDanse looked over the rail.\n\n“I should check the duty roster.”\n\n“At twenty-two hundred?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Coward.”\n\nHis head snapped toward me.\n\n“What did you say?”\n\n“You heard me.”\n\n“I am not a coward.”\n\n“Then stay.”\n\nI expected him to leave out of principle.\n\nInstead he sat on the bench beside me.\n\n“Fine.”\n\n“Outstanding.”\n\n“Don’t start.”\n\nWe stayed.\n\nThe conversation wandered into meaningless things, which somehow felt more intimate than confession.\n\nWorst pre-War food we had eaten. Danse claimed canned imitation crab was a crime against humanity. I argued that the Brotherhood should revise its mission statement accordingly.\n\nFavorite weapon modifications. Best places to sleep in the field. Whether a mirelurk counted as seafood.\n\n“It lives in the water,” I said.\n\n“So do things I would not eat.”\n\n“That is cowardice again.”\n\n“That word does not mean what you think it means.”\n\nAt some point our knees touched.\n\nNeither of us moved.\n\nThe contact lasted long enough to become intentional.\n\nDanse looked down.\n\nThen at me.\n\n“I meant what I said earlier.”\n\n“About not romancing me?”\n\nHe shut his eyes briefly.\n\n“No. About enjoying working with you.”\n\n“Oh.”\n\nHis voice lowered.\n\n“It’s… comfortable.”\n\nFor Danse, that might have been a declaration.\n\nI swallowed.\n\n“I feel the same.”\n\nHe nodded.\n\nOur knees remained together.\n\nNo kiss.\n\nNo dramatic confession.\n\nJust the Prydwen humming around us and a soldier who feared attachment allowing one small point of contact to remain.\n\nEventually Danse stood.\n\n“Get some sleep.”\n\n“There’s the romance technique.”\n\nHe groaned.\n\nI grinned.\n\nAs he walked away he called back, “Good night.”\n\nNot civilian.\n\nNot soldier.\n\nJust good night.\n\nI sat there another minute smiling into my beer like an idiot.\n\nHis next attempt at flirting involved a torque wrench.\n\nI was replacing the recoil compensator on a combat rifle when Danse appeared at the bench and set a tool beside my elbow.\n\n“You’ll get better leverage with this.”\n\nI looked at the wrench.\n\nThen at him.\n\n“Did you come all the way across the Prydwen to critique my leverage?”\n\n“I was passing through.”\n\n“You are carrying two cups of coffee.”\n\nHe looked down as if surprised to find them.\n\n“One is for you.”\n\n“Is it drinkable?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“That was not a confident answer.”\n\n“I added sugar.”\n\n“How much?”\n\nDanse became suspiciously interested in the rifle.\n\nI tasted it.\n\nThe coffee was still strong enough to strip paint, but someone had clearly attempted mercy.\n\n“Danse.”\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“You remembered how I take it.”\n\nHis ears reddened.\n\n“I remember operational preferences.”\n\n“Sugar is tactical now?”\n\n“It affects morale.”\n\nI laughed so hard I had to set the cup down.\n\nDanse crossed his arms.\n\n“I fail to see what’s amusing.”\n\n“You have been flirting with me for weeks and somehow you’re doing it through logistics.”\n\nHis entire face stopped.\n\nI stopped laughing.\n\nOh.\n\nThere are moments when the Commonwealth seems to hold its breath just to see what stupid thing I will say next.\n\nDanse stared at me.\n\n“I have not—”\n\n“You don’t have to—”\n\n“I mean, I wasn’t—”\n\n“Right.”\n\n“Not intentionally.”\n\nThat shut us both up.\n\nHis eyes widened a fraction as he heard himself.\n\nI leaned against the bench.\n\n“Not intentionally?”\n\nDanse looked as though he would have preferred another super mutant battle.\n\n“I enjoy your company.”\n\nThe words emerged with the rigid precision of a formal report.\n\n“I had gathered that.”\n\n“I respect your judgment.”\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\n“Frequently.”\n\n“Generous.”\n\n“And I…”\n\nHe stopped.\n\nHis gaze dropped to the coffee cup.\n\n“I notice when you’re not around.”\n\nThe room suddenly felt too small.\n\nI let the teasing fall away.\n\n“I notice too.”\n\nDanse looked up.\n\nWe stood on opposite sides of a workbench covered in springs, screws, and a half-disassembled rifle.\n\nIt was not romantic by any reasonable standard.\n\nIt felt more intimate than a candlelit dinner could have.\n\nHe reached toward me, hesitated, then picked up the torque wrench instead.\n\n“This fastener is going to strip.”\n\nBefore he could escape into the fastener, I noticed a small cloth-wrapped bundle near his elbow.\n\n“What’s that?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“Suspicious answer.”\n\n“It’s a component.”\n\n“For?”\n\nDanse hesitated, then unfolded the cloth. Inside was a polished beam focuser, cleaner than anything available in the common parts bins.\n\nI stared at it.\n\n“You requisitioned this.”\n\n“I located it.”\n\n“With paperwork?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“For my rifle.”\n\n“For Righteous Authority.”\n\n“My rifle.”\n\nHe folded his arms. “You’ve been complaining about energy dispersion.”\n\n“I complained once.”\n\n“You were correct.”\n\nI picked up the focuser. “Is this a gift?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Danse.”\n\n“It improves mission effectiveness.”\n\n“Is that what Brotherhood officers call flowers?”\n\nHis entire face turned red.\n\n“I have never given anyone flowers.”\n\n“I believe you.”\n\nHe reached for the part. I pulled it out of range.\n\n“Thank you.”\n\nThe embarrassment eased enough for him to meet my eyes.\n\n“You’re welcome.”\n\nThere it was again. Care translated into equipment because equipment had specifications and affection did not.\n\nI could work with that.\n\nI installed the new focuser while Danse watched over my shoulder.\n\n“Quarter turn,” he said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You’re over-tightening it.”\n\n“I am not.”\n\nHe reached around me and put his hand over mine on the tool.\n\nThe correction became unnecessary almost immediately.\n\nNeither of us moved.\n\nHis chest was warm against my back. His breath caught near my ear. For a man who could issue orders under gunfire without hesitation, Danse had no procedure for standing too close to someone he wanted.\n\n“Quarter turn?” I asked quietly.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You stopped turning.”\n\n“I noticed.”\n\nHis hand left mine with visible reluctance.\n\nThe rifle, at least, had the decency to remain uncomplicated.\n\nI stared.\n\nHe stared back.\n\n“That’s your retreat?”\n\n“I don’t know what you mean.”\n\n“Coward.”\n\nHis mouth opened.\n\nThen he laughed.\n\nActually laughed, head dropping for a second as the sound escaped him.\n\nIt was warm and rough and so rare I forgot the comeback I had prepared.\n\nWhen he recovered, he slid the wrench toward me.\n\n“Use the correct tool.”\n\nI took it.\n\nOur fingers brushed.\n\nNeither of us pretended not to notice.\n\nThe rifle took another twenty minutes.\n\nThe conversation took considerably longer.\n\nThree days after the incident with the wrench, Danse found me sorting ammunition at the workbench.\n\n\"I have a question,\" he said.\n\n\"Is it a test?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Are you certain? Because with you those categories overlap.\"\n\nHe set a small box of cleaned bolts beside me. He had labeled them by size and condition.\n\nThe case latch caught as I tried to slide it into place. Danse noticed before I swore at it.\n\n“Stop. The hinge is bent.”\n\n“I'm beginning to think the entire Commonwealth is bent.”\n\n“That is not an adequate inspection category.”\n\nHe took the case, checked the latch, then set it aside with a scrap of red cloth tied around its handle. He didn't try to force it shut. Every mistake at a bench had consequences, and I liked that he remembered that even when he was flustered.\n\n“Should I fetch another?” I asked.\n\n“Please.”\n\nI stopped moving. He noticed.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You said please.”\n\n“I am capable of courtesy.”\n\n“Right, but you usually issue it at parade-ground volume.”\n\nHis ears colored. “You are making this unnecessarily difficult.”\n\n“Only because you're worth annoying.”\n\nDanse opened his mouth, closed it, and checked the safe case twice before he spoke again. The thoroughness was familiar; the uncertainty was not.\n\n“I wasn't trained for conversations in which the answer cannot be evaluated against a written standard,” he admitted.\n\n“Most of us aren't.”\n\n“Then I would prefer to ask plainly, even if the answer is complicated.”\n\nHe set the cartridges aside and faced me with a gravity that might have been ridiculous if it weren't so sincere. I waited for the question. I knew whatever came next would sound like Danse, not like someone serenading me or handing me a parable about love.\n\n\"Are you currently involved with anyone?\"\n\nI nearly dropped a cartridge.\n\n\"That's direct.\"\n\n\"I thought you preferred directness.\"\n\n\"I do.\" I set the cartridge down. \"No, not at the moment. But there's something you should know if this is going where I think it might.\"\n\nThe tips of his ears turned red. \"And where is that?\"\n\n\"Somewhere with far more paperwork than either of us expected.\"\n\nHe folded his arms.\n\n\"I'm ethically non-monogamous,\" I said. \"I prefer polyamory. For me that means I might have more than one loving relationship, with everyone fully informed and consenting. No hidden partners. No rules made behind somebody's back. And it means you would always get to decide what you were comfortable with.\"\n\nDanse stared at the box of bolts.\n\n\"I understand the definition,\" he said. \"I'm considering the implications.\"\n\n\"That is an extremely Danse answer.\"\n\n\"Would you expect me to share information about our relationship with other people?\"\n\n\"Not your private history. We would decide together what stays between us, what gets disclosed to someone directly affected, and what is nobody else's business. I won't share your trauma as a relationship update.\"\n\nHe gave that the thought it deserved.\n\n\"Would I be required to pursue other relationships?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Would you expect me not to experience jealousy?\"\n\n\"Also no. I'd expect you to talk to me rather than turning it into an order.\"\n\nHe looked almost offended. \"I don't issue orders about personal matters.\"\n\n\"You told me to eat lunch yesterday.\"\n\n\"That was a health recommendation.\"\n\n\"At parade-ground volume.\"\n\nA reluctant breath escaped him. It might have been a laugh.\n\n\"I was taught loyalty is singular,\" he said. \"One chain of command. One mission. One oath. I need to think about whether I've mistaken those rules for rules about people.\"\n\n\"You're allowed to think.\"\n\n\"And to say no?\"\n\n\"Yes. Even to me.\"\n\nHe nodded. \"Then I would like to keep seeing where this leads. I won't ask you to promise exclusivity. If my feelings change, I'll tell you rather than pretend.\"\n\nI felt the relief like someone had loosened a strap I'd forgotten was tight.\n\n\"One request,\" he said.\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"No critical relationship discussions while handling live ammunition.\"\n\n\"Fair.\"\n\nHe slid the ammunition case out of my reach. Then, very carefully, he placed his hand on the bench beside mine. An invitation, not a command. I let our little fingers touch.\n\nThe next time he told me to use the correct tool, I made a point of asking whether he had a form for romantic incompetence.\n\nHe said, \"Several.\"\n\nI believed him.","word_count":2517},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-32":{"number":32,"title":"Civilian Clothes","subtitle":"What the Armor Leaves Behind · Chapter 32 of 40","content":"Danse owned exactly one outfit that was not Brotherhood-issued.\n\nIt was ugly.\n\nI loved it immediately.\n\nHe emerged from the back room at Bravo wearing faded work pants, a brown shirt, and a pre-War jacket we had traded for at Bunker Hill. Without the uniform, without armor, without rank insignia, he looked like an entirely different man.\n\nHe looked deeply uncomfortable.\n\n“Well?” he asked.\n\nI stared.\n\n“That bad?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You’re staring.”\n\n“I do that sometimes.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“You’re enjoying this.”\n\n“Immensely.”\n\nHe looked down at himself.\n\n“The jacket is impractical.”\n\n“It has pockets.”\n\n“Not enough.”\n\n“No garment has enough pockets for you.”\n\n“That’s a design failure.”\n\nWe were going to Diamond City.\n\nDanse had resisted for days because the Brotherhood still operated nearby and because being recognized felt dangerous.\n\nBut Bravo needed supplies. More importantly, Danse needed to walk through a place where nobody cared about his former rank.\n\nThe market was crowded.\n\nHe stayed close.\n\nNot protectively this time.\n\nUneasily.\n\nPeople bumped him without apology. Vendors yelled prices. A child nearly ran into him carrying noodles.\n\nDanse looked more overwhelmed than he had during some firefights.\n\n“You all right?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Liar.”\n\n“I’m adjusting.”\n\n“To civilians?”\n\n“To not having a visible role.”\n\nThat made sense.\n\nA Brotherhood uniform told people what he was before he spoke.\n\nNow he had to be only himself.\n\nWe bought ammunition, soap, coffee, and a new pan.\n\nAt the kitchenware stall Danse checked the handle of the pan twice, then asked the woman selling it whether she could show him the other side of the hinge.\n\n“You think I'm trying to cheat you?” she demanded.\n\n“No, ma'am. I think the rivet may be loose.”\n\nShe turned the pan over, squinted, and gave it a hard shake. The handle moved.\n\n“Well, damn.” She dug another pan out of a crate. “You want the good one or a discount on the bad one?”\n\n“The good one.”\n\n“Then stop looking like I'm charging you for a suit of power armor. That's six caps.”\n\nHe counted them carefully. When she held out her hand for the payment, he hesitated. I could see him measuring his voice, looking for the rank or designation that would make an ordinary transaction feel safer.\n\n“Thank you,” he said finally.\n\n“Enjoy your breakfast, whatever it is.”\n\nWe were halfway down the market aisle before he spoke again.\n\n“I nearly told her to inspect the rest of the stock.”\n\n“You would've been right about the rivet.”\n\n“That doesn't entitle me to command her shop.”\n\n“Nope.”\n\nHe seemed disappointed and oddly proud of the observation at the same time.\n\nA few stalls later I reached for a scarf from a stack of woolens. Danse steadied the display before it could fall. His arm brushed the small of my back, deliberate enough that I knew he hadn't needed to reach quite so far.\n\n“Careful,” he murmured.\n\n“Is that an order?”\n\n“An assessment.”\n\n“Of the scarves?”\n\nHe looked at my mouth and then away. “Of the situation.”\n\nMy pulse did a thoroughly embarrassing thing. He cleared his throat and began examining the stitchwork with the gravity of a field inspection. I let him, because I wanted to see how long he could keep pretending he hadn't been flirting.\n\nAt a clothing stall, Danse stopped beside a dark red scarf.\n\nI saw the look.\n\n“You like it.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You absolutely do.”\n\n“It serves no purpose.”\n\n“It keeps your neck warm.”\n\n“My jacket does that.”\n\n“Then it serves a second purpose.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“Looking good.”\n\nHe stared at me.\n\nI bought it.\n\nDanse protested for six straight minutes.\n\nThen wore it all the way home.\n\nAt Bravo, he stood in front of the cracked mirror adjusting the knot.\n\n“You’re smiling,” I said.\n\n“I am not.”\n\n“You are.”\n\n“It’s functional.”\n\n“It’s red.”\n\n“That is a color.”\n\n“It’s your color now.”\n\nHe looked at my reflection in the mirror.\n\n“What does that mean?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“That’s suspicious.”\n\nI stepped behind him and fixed the scarf where it had folded under the jacket collar.\n\nMy fingers brushed the side of his neck.\n\nDanse went still.\n\nThe mirror caught his eyes meeting mine.\n\nThe room changed.\n\nI let my hand remain there.\n\nHe covered it with his.\n\n“You know,” I said softly, “you’re allowed to keep things because you like them.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You’re allowed to look good because you want to.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You’re allowed—”\n\nDanse turned and kissed me before I could finish.\n\nIt was not careful like the first time.\n\nStill gentle.\n\nBut no hesitation.\n\nHis hands settled at my waist. Mine caught in the front of his ridiculous civilian jacket.\n\nWhen we separated, I was breathing harder.\n\nDanse looked startled by himself.\n\n“Sorry.”\n\n“No.”\n\nHe blinked.\n\n“Right. No apologizing.”\n\n“Correct.”\n\nHis forehead rested against mine.\n\n“I like the scarf,” he admitted.\n\nI laughed.\n\n“That was harder to say than kissing me, wasn’t it?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Hopeless.”\n\n“Apparently.”\n\nThe next morning he wore it again.\n\nHe pretended that meant nothing.\n\nSo did I.\n\nWe were both lying.\n\nDiamond City recognized him eventually.\n\nNot everyone.\n\nOne Brotherhood caravan guard did.\n\nDanse and I were leaving the market with a bag of coffee beans and two replacement fuses when the guard stepped from beside the gate.\n\nHis eyes moved from the red scarf to Danse’s face.\n\n“Paladin?”\n\nDanse stopped.\n\nThe title hit him like a hand against the chest.\n\nThe guard went pale as recognition became memory.\n\n“Sir, I—”\n\nDanse’s hand moved slightly toward mine, then stopped before touching.\n\n“I’m not a paladin.”\n\nThe guard looked around as if saying the wrong thing might summon Maxson.\n\n“I heard you were dead.”\n\n“So did I.”\n\nIt was the driest thing Danse had ever said.\n\nI nearly choked.\n\nThe guard did not know whether he was allowed to laugh.\n\nDanse saved him.\n\n“You should return to your caravan.”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\nHe took one step, stopped.\n\n“Sorry. Not sir.”\n\nDanse’s expression softened by a fraction.\n\n“Be safe.”\n\nThe guard nodded and left.\n\nWe made it outside the gate before Danse sat on a concrete barrier.\n\nI put the supplies down beside him.\n\n“You okay?”\n\n“No.”\n\nGood answer.\n\nI waited.\n\n“He looked afraid of me.”\n\n“He looked afraid of the situation.”\n\n“I used to inspect his unit.”\n\n“You remember him?”\n\n“Knight Vargas. Poor trigger discipline when he was nervous. Improved after his second deployment.”\n\nOf course Danse remembered.\n\n“He probably thought I was going to arrest him for talking to me.”\n\n“You are currently wearing a scarf I bought because it was pretty.”\n\n“That does not eliminate the possibility of arrest.”\n\n“It substantially lowers the intimidation factor.”\n\nHe looked down at the scarf.\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nThen he went quiet again.\n\n“I wanted to ask how the Prydwen is.”\n\n“Why didn’t you?”\n\n“Because he would have had to choose between answering me and following his orders.”\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n“That was kind.”\n\n“It was practical.”\n\n“No.”\n\nDanse gave me a sideways look.\n\n“Still doing that?” I asked.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Renaming kindness so it doesn’t embarrass you.”\n\nHe looked back toward the city gate.\n\n“Maybe.”\n\nWe stayed until the moment passed.\n\nThen I picked up the bag.\n\n“Come on. We still need soap.”\n\nDanse stood.\n\n“Two bars.”\n\n“Three.”\n\n“Why three?”\n\n“Because normal people stock extras.”\n\n“We are not normal.”\n\n“Fair.”\n\nAt the clothing stall, the same vendor who had sold me the scarf waved us over.\n\n“Got something for your man.”\n\nDanse visibly regretted having a face.\n\n“My—”\n\nThe vendor held up a dark wool sweater.\n\n“Warm. No insignia. Actual sleeves.”\n\nI touched the fabric.\n\nDanse checked the seams.\n\nOf course he did.\n\n“How much?” he asked.\n\nThe vendor named a price.\n\nDanse negotiated with ruthless precision until she called him a bastard and reduced it by fifteen caps.\n\nHe wore the sweater home.\n\nHalfway to Bravo, rain started.\n\nHe pulled the red scarf higher around his neck.\n\nI smiled.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You look like a person going home from the market.”\n\nHis brow furrowed.\n\n“I am a person going home from the market.”\n\nExactly.\n\nHe realized what he had said a few steps later.\n\nHis pace slowed.\n\nI did not comment.\n\nNeither did he.\n\nBut his hand found mine.\n\nThe trip home took longer because Danse kept discovering that civilian clothing had consequences.\n\nThe jacket caught on a rusted fence.\n\nThe work pants lacked reinforced knees.\n\nThe sweater was, according to him, “thermally adequate but tactically irresponsible.”\n\n“You bought it.”\n\n“I was cold.”\n\n“You negotiated fifteen caps off it like a military campaign.”\n\n“Overpricing should be challenged.”\n\nAt a roadside checkpoint run by settlers, nobody saluted him.\n\nNobody called him Paladin.\n\nA guard simply glanced at our bags and said, “Evening, Danse.”\n\nHe stopped walking.\n\nThe guard looked worried. “Did I get the name wrong?”\n\n“No.”\n\nDanse’s answer came slowly.\n\n“No. You didn’t.”\n\nWe continued down the road.\n\nA few minutes later he touched the red scarf at his throat.\n\n“What?” I asked.\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“You look like you’re thinking hard enough to damage the sweater.”\n\nHe ignored that.\n\n“It was strange.”\n\n“Not being called Paladin?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Bad?”\n\nHe took several steps before answering.\n\n“No.”\n\nThat single word seemed to surprise him more than it surprised me.\n\nAt Bravo, Danse emptied his pockets onto the table.\n\nTwo fuses. A screwdriver. Three screws he had apparently acquired without noticing. A folded market receipt. One bottle cap flattened almost perfectly round.\n\nI picked it up.\n\n“What’s this?”\n\n“Trash.”\n\n“You carried trash home?”\n\nHe took it from me, then hesitated.\n\n“A child at the gate gave it to me.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“He said it was lucky.”\n\nI waited.\n\nDanse stared at the cap.\n\n“You kept it.”\n\n“It was in my pocket.”\n\n“You put it there.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe set it carefully on the shelf beside the detective novel.\n\nThe man who once catalogued only equipment had brought home a useless piece of metal because a child thought it mattered.\n\nI decided not to tease him.\n\nThat was how much I loved him.\n\nBefore putting the clothes away, he caught his reflection in the cracked bunker mirror.\n\nNo armor. No insignia. Red scarf, ugly sweater, tired eyes.\n\nHe studied himself longer than usual.\n\n“Recognize him?” I asked.\n\n“Not entirely.”\n\n“Do you have to?”\n\nHe considered.\n\n“No.”\n\nThen he smiled at the reflection, faintly, like an introduction.\n\nThe mirror did not give him an answer. It only gave him a face he was learning to claim without a uniform telling him what it meant.\n\nThat night he folded the sweater and placed it in the same drawer as his Brotherhood undersuit.\n\nOld uniform beneath.\n\nCivilian clothes above.\n\nNot replacement.\n\nLayering.\n\nI watched him close the drawer.\n\n“Red tomorrow?” I asked.\n\n“The scarf is useful.”\n\n“Sure.”\n\n“It is.”\n\n“Danse.”\n\nHe sighed.\n\n“I like it.”\n\n“Say the whole thing.”\n\n“I like the red scarf you bought me because it looks good.”\n\nI put a hand to my chest.\n\n“Growth.”\n\nHe kissed me before I could get worse.\n\nI considered this positive reinforcement.\n","word_count":1822},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-42":{"number":42,"title":"Letter from the Old World","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass two","content":"The envelope was made from a page torn out of an old technical manual.\n\nIt arrived at Brasswatch Yard wrapped around a small packet of electrical connectors, delivered by a trader who did not know the sender's name. The paper had been folded so precisely that I could almost hear Danse correcting someone's technique.\n\nHe recognized the handwriting before he opened it.\n\nHis face went still.\n\n\"Haylen?\"\n\nI looked at the packet, then at him.\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nHe carried it into the workshop and shut the door. I stayed outside. It was not an act of generosity; it was an effort not to confuse my curiosity with his permission.\n\nFive minutes passed.\n\nThen ten.\n\nAt last, he opened the door.\n\nDanse kept the letter in his right hand. I could see the pressure of his thumb where the paper folded; I couldn't see what Haylen had written, and I didn't try.\n\"The connectors are the correct gauge for the purifier,\" I said, pointing to the parts visible on the bench. \"We can check those without discussing the letter.\"\n\"I am aware.\"\n\"Okay.\"\nHe stayed in the doorway long enough that I wondered whether he expected an argument. Then he stepped aside and let me reach the service log.\nAster stopped by with a sample tray and a schedule from the market committee. The people using Brasswatch water had questions about the flow rate, not the contents of a former Brotherhood paladin's correspondence.\n\"If the indicator turns green, that doesn't certify drinkable water,\" she said. \"Run it at load, then test the output. Put the dates where everyone can see them.\"\nDanse examined the voltage specification. \"We should isolate the second circuit until the first passes.\"\n\"That would help,\" Aster said. \"Especially if you write it down.\"\nI watched him amend the log in the clipped, careful handwriting he used when a decision mattered.\nAfter she left, Danse closed the drawer with Haylen's letter inside. The sound was soft, not final.\n\"You don't have to tell me what she said,\" I reminded him.\n\"I know.\" He looked toward the silent purifier. \"It is difficult to distinguish withholding information from choosing not to surrender it.\"\n\"Maybe the difference is whose information it is.\"\nHe considered that without answering immediately. Finally he moved a stool beside the workbench for me.\nThe purifier remained untested. The sealed letter remained private. I sat with him through the quiet, not to make either problem vanish, but because he had asked me to stay.\n\n\"Jasper?\"\n\nI went inside.\n\nDanse stood beside the bench with a folded note in one hand. The packet of connectors lay unopened. His shoulders were set as if he expected an inspection.\n\n\"It's from Haylen,\" he said.\n\n\"Do you want to tell me what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe handed me the paper.\n\nThe message was short. Haylen wrote that the Brotherhood had changed patrol routes near a settlement Danse once supplied. Some residents were afraid a former Paladin might try to use the settlement's old radio frequency. The letter did not ask Danse to return. It asked him to avoid the channel until the people who lived there had chosen a safer way to communicate.\n\nAt the end, in smaller writing, she'd added:\n\nI HOPE YOU ARE WELL. PLEASE DON'T ANSWER THROUGH THE OLD NET.\n\nI read it twice.\n\n\"She wants you safe,\" I said.\n\n\"She wants civilians safe.\"\n\n\"Both can be true.\"\n\nDanse did not answer.\n\nHe took the note and placed it beside the purifier ledger.\n\n\"The old net is the most reliable frequency I know.\"\n\n\"That doesn't make it the right one.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe sounded irritated.\n\n\"Danse.\"\n\n\"I know, Jasper.\"\n\nHe moved to the workbench and began sorting the connectors by size, though they were already sorted. The motion was familiar enough to make my chest ache.\n\nI waited until he stopped.\n\n\"What are you afraid of?\" I asked.\n\n\"That if I don't respond, she'll think I have forgotten her.\"\n\n\"And if you respond through the channel?\"\n\n\"People could be endangered.\"\n\n\"Then the answer isn't a radio message.\"\n\nHe looked at me, the conflict plain.\n\n\"She's one of the few people who knew me before everything changed.\"\n\nI stood beside him, close enough that our sleeves touched.\n\n\"You don't have to stop caring about her to respect the boundary she's giving you.\"\n\nHis breath caught.\n\n\"That's not what I meant.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe looked down at his hands.\n\n\"I was trained to answer a call. Silence meant failure. Sometimes abandonment.\"\n\n\"Then maybe you need a different way to show you're still here.\"\n\nHe thought about it.\n\nThe following morning, we went to Neri with the note. Not to ask permission to answer Haylen, but to ask whether the Threadway courier network could deliver a sealed letter without identifying the settlement or exposing the person who had carried the first message.\n\nNeri listened and shook their head.\n\n\"Not without the recipient agreeing to receive it.\"\n\nDanse's jaw tightened.\n\n\"Then how do people communicate across the doors?\"\n\n\"They arrange it,\" Neri said. \"Or they wait.\"\n\n\"Waiting is unreliable.\"\n\n\"Sometimes it is the only honest answer.\"\n\nHe looked as though he wanted to argue. Instead, he asked for a blank page.\n\nWe returned to Brasswatch.\n\nHe wrote the letter slowly. I did not read over his shoulder. When he finished, he folded it and placed it in a plain envelope with no identifying marks on the outside.\n\nHe told me what he'd written only after he sealed it.\n\nHe had thanked Haylen for the warning. He had said he would not use the old network. He had told her that his life was different, and that the people who once served beside him still mattered. He had not asked her to put herself at risk for a reply.\n\n\"And now?\" I asked.\n\n\"Now I wait until there's a safe invitation.\"\n\nHe looked almost ill from the effort.\n\nI found two cups and poured water from the safe hand pump.\n\n\"That sounds difficult.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"Welcome to the club.\"\n\nHe took the cup.\n\n\"You make light of everything.\"\n\n\"Not everything. Just the parts that might crush us if we never laugh.\"\n\nA small smile reached his mouth.\n\nBefore we returned to the purifier, Renn found Danse studying the sealed letter in his desk drawer.\n\n“Don't use the old courier seal,” they warned. “The mark on the outside is enough to tell somebody which network carried it.”\n\nDanse looked up sharply. “You recognized it?”\n\n“I recognized that you were hiding the mark under your hand. Different thing.”\n\nHe didn't enjoy the distinction. His first instinct was to ask exactly how much Renn knew and whether the letter needed to be confiscated. I saw his fingers close around the envelope.\n\nRenn had not asked to become an intelligence officer. They were carrying a worn connector and trying to keep the workbench safe.\n\n“The return label can be ordinary,” they said. “Use a permitted merchant address if the sender consents. No reason to put anyone else at risk because you want an answer today.”\n\nDanse set the letter down.\n\n“I am not asking you to handle it,” he said.\n\n“I wasn't offering.”\n\nAfter Renn left, he looked at the blank envelope for a long moment.\n\n“I used to think an unanswered message was a failure to maintain contact.”\n\n“Sometimes it's somebody choosing the safest way to live.”\n\nHe nodded. He put the original in a locked personal drawer, not the workshop records, and wrote a note reminding himself to ask before forwarding any part of Haylen's words.\n\nThat evening he repaired the purifier's cracked gasket instead of drafting another letter. The water ran clear through the test line; Renn recorded the measurement and signed it.\n\nThere was still a letter in the drawer. There was also a repaired machine. He had no need to make one stand in for the other.\n\n\"Are we getting a new parts supplier?\" they asked.\n\n\"Not yet,\" he said.\n\nRenn held up the connector packet Haylen had sent. \"These might fit the old regulator. I wasn't going to open them without asking.\"\n\nDanse thanked them. They compared the connectors with the workshop's inventory, noting which ones could be safely tested and which would need a separate circuit check. He didn't use the parts merely because they had come from someone he trusted. A good source could still send the wrong component.\n\n\"Should we credit the sender in the public repairs ledger?\" Renn asked.\n\nDanse's hand paused.\n\n\"No personal name,\" he said. \"Record the specification and the inspection result. If the supplier ever wants to be publicly identified, that decision should be theirs.\"\n\nRenn nodded and wrote *private donation, pending test* instead. I saw a little relief move through Danse's shoulders.\n\nHe wasn't closing off Haylen's part in his life. He was keeping a correspondence safe until she could choose another way to receive it.\n\n\"You can miss somebody without transmitting on an unsafe channel,\" I said when Renn left.\n\n\"I am discovering that silence requires substantially more discipline than I was taught.\"\n\n\"Well, there's something they don't put on the recruitment poster.\"\n\nHe gave me a long look, then returned to the actual repair instructions. The connector remained on the bench until it could be tested. The letter stayed sealed.\n\nWe worked the rest of the day on the purifier's intake pipe. Severus had warned us about residues that appeared after the system sat idle. Danse had taken the criticism seriously enough to redesign the testing schedule. Every sample would be collected and labeled before anyone declared the water safe.\n\nAt noon, Tamsin stopped by with a list of residents who needed deliveries until the repairs were complete. Danse read the list carefully.\n\n\"Three households require assistance carrying water,\" he said.\n\n\"Four,\" I corrected. \"One person doesn't want their name announced publicly.\"\n\nHe looked up.\n\n\"Then we can arrange a route without displaying it.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"You're learning.\"\n\n\"I was already capable of discretion.\"\n\n\"Understatement of the century.\"\n\nHe shook his head, but there was warmth in it.\n\nThe letter stayed in a locked drawer. Not because he had stopped wanting to send it, but because wanting didn't give him the right to use a channel that might harm someone else.\n\nThat evening he found me on the workshop steps.\n\n\"Could I ask you something?\" he said.\n\n\"Always.\"\n\nHe sat beside me, the old scarf loose around his neck.\n\n\"Do you think caring about someone far away makes what I have with you less important?\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"No. You know what I believe about loving more than one person. But even outside romance, care isn't a resource we use up by remembering people.\"\n\nHe nodded slowly.\n\n\"I still have trouble believing that.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps I should say something I do believe.\" He looked directly at me. \"I want you here tonight. Not because Haylen isn't here. Because you are.\"\n\nWarmth settled behind my ribs.\n\n\"That I can do.\"\n\nHe touched my cheek with one hand, waiting for the small answering movement that told him I wanted the kiss. I leaned into his palm.\n\nHis mouth was warm. His beard scratched lightly against my skin. He was still learning how to be gentle without making gentleness feel like a protocol.\n\nWhen we parted, he kept his forehead against mine for a moment.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he said.\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For not asking me to forget.\"\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\nThe next morning, the letter was still in the drawer.\n\nThe purifier was still offline.\n\nBoth were unfinished.\n\nFor the first time, Danse seemed willing to let unfinished mean that he could keep caring without taking the wrong action simply to make the waiting stop.","word_count":1954},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-48":{"number":48,"title":"The Night the Lights Went Out","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","content":"The generator failed with a sound like a very large person clearing their throat.\n\nThen the entire eastern side of Lanternwell Square went dark.\n\nI was at Brasswatch Yard with Danse, checking the volunteer repair schedule. One moment the workshop lights were humming; the next they were gone. Outside, somebody shouted. A few seconds later the emergency lantern by the door came on.\n\nDanse was already moving.\n\n\"Stay back from the panel,\" he said.\n\n\"I wasn't planning to touch it.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nHe opened the insulated access cover and looked at the gauges. The smell of overheated wiring hung in the air, sharp and unpleasant. The generator had been carrying extra load since the storm flooded the lower service route. We knew it needed inspection. We hadn't expected it to fail tonight.\n\nA runner arrived from the square.\n\n\"The hall's dark. People are sheltering from the weather.\"\n\nDanse looked at the panel.\n\n\"I can restore power.\"\n\n\"When?\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"Not until I know what failed.\"\n\nThat was the right answer, though it hurt to hear it.\n\nWe brought the portable lanterns to the hall. Maui was already carrying blankets to the west benches. Katara was checking that the children had their caregivers nearby. Neri had a paper list of people who had chosen to wait there, with no public record of private destinations.\n\nI helped set lanterns where they wouldn't block the exit.\n\nDanse spoke to the volunteers.\n\n\"We need a trained person at the generator, two people to watch the backup lamps, and someone to check the cables for water damage. No one enters the electrical room without protective equipment.\"\n\nHe didn't assign people by pointing at them. He asked who was trained and available.\n\nRenn raised a hand.\n\n\"I can check the cable run. I trained on that system last week.\"\n\nDanse looked at them.\n\n\"With me.\"\n\nAster volunteered for the backup lamps. Pip offered to maintain the clear path, then asked to be relieved after an hour.\n\nEverything moved more slowly than a military emergency would have.\n\nIt also moved with people who knew what they'd agreed to do.\n\nWe found the fault near the old canal service junction. Water had reached an improperly sealed conduit. One breaker had tripped, and the generator's load control had tried to compensate until it shut down.\n\nDanse stared at the panel.\n\n\"I approved that conduit inspection last month.\"\n\n\"Was it completed?\" I asked.\n\nHe checked the log.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nI felt a cold knot in my stomach.\n\n\"Then we document that.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nThere would be time later for accountability. Right now we needed safe lights, dry equipment, and a plan that didn't make the next repair more dangerous than the outage itself.\n\nRenn tested the replacement cable while Danse checked the voltage. Aster reported that two backup lamps were losing power. We moved the remaining lights to the highest-traffic routes and made sure people knew which corridors were still dark.\n\nNo one was ordered to stay in the hall.\n\nNo one was forced through a Threadway.\n\nNear midnight, a deep note sounded from the direction of Tideglass. The second bell.\n\nDanse looked toward the door.\n\n\"Pressure in the lower route,\" he said.\n\n\"Could it affect the generator?\"\n\n\"Possibly. We should assume the service junction is still wet until we test it.\"\n\nAster handed him a folded delivery sheet. Beren had drawn a thick line around the north approach and crossed out the service corridor. Edda's initials appeared beside the turn that needed extra clearance.\n\n“Water for the east shelter,” Aster said. “Amel says they need the first load tonight. The cart's coming around the long way, but it still has to pass this door.”\n\nDanse studied the map. The drawing was rough, more useful than any elegant plan I'd seen in the workshop office. Someone had marked the narrow step with a black square. The crossing was too tight for a cart if we stacked the spare cable reels beside it.\n\n“Those reels have to move,” he said.\n\nRenn looked over his shoulder. “Not into the electrical room.”\n\n“Agreed. We can put them in the dry store—after checking the exit remains clear.”\n\nI watched him start to divide the job, then stop.\n\n“Aster, do you have the clearance measurements?”\n\n“Edda checked them with Beren. I've got the note.”\n\n“Good. Show us the dry store and tell me whether a cart can turn there.”\n\nIt was not the command voice he'd once used when the answer was already written in his head. He still sounded like a soldier, and he still made every sentence as precise as he could. But he was waiting for a volunteer's measurements before moving anything.\n\nOutside, the square remained dark. I could hear water sloshing in the cart as it approached the corner. The sound did something to me that the generator's silence hadn't: it reminded me that the numbers Danse was looking at belonged to people who were thirsty.\n\n“Will you be at the panel when the cart passes?” I asked.\n\n“No. Renn can watch the indicator. I'll help keep the route clear.”\n\n“You're sure?”\n\nHe glanced at the drawing. “The man delivering water has already changed his route for us. We can change one doorway for him.”\n\nHe folded the note carefully and handed it back. The power could wait for a safe test. The water could not wait for his pride.\n\nHe refused to restart the system immediately.\n\nA few people complained.\n\nOne man shouted that the old Brotherhood would have had the lights back already.\n\nDanse looked at him.\n\n\"The old Brotherhood might have ordered someone into a flooded electrical room. That doesn't make it safe.\"\n\nThe man stopped.\n\nI watched Danse turn back to the panel.\n\nIt was a small victory, but one he'd earned with work rather than a speech.\n\nAt two in the morning Aster returned from the shelter carrying the last charged emergency lamp.\n\n\"One of the halls is too dark,\" they said. \"The lanterns are bright enough near the main door, but the side passage doesn't have any light.\"\n\nDanse checked the diagram.\n\n\"We could move one from the equipment room.\"\n\nRenn shook their head. \"We need that light to see the shutdown indicator if the backup system fails.\"\n\nI looked between them.\n\n\"Then what's the other option?\"\n\nNobody answered immediately.\n\nRenn brought the load sheet over from the generator panel. In the light of the emergency lantern, the numbers looked like little black insects crawling down the page.\n\n\"We can keep the clinic refrigerator on backup,\" they said. \"Not the repair presses. Not tonight.\"\n\nDanse took the sheet. \"The press line could be shut down by section.\"\n\n\"It already is,\" Renn answered. \"You don't need to go back and switch it off a second time.\"\n\nI watched his expression. He hated being told he was too late to solve something, even when the problem had been solved well.\n\nAster came in from the hall with a damp coat and a question about the east exit. Someone had moved a spare crate to make space for a lantern, and now the route was too narrow for the cart carrying drinking water.\n\nDanse turned toward the door.\n\nRenn stopped him. \"I'll take the panel. You can check the hall with Aster. One problem at a time.\"\n\nHe hesitated, then handed back the sheet.\n\nI followed him into the corridor. The floor felt colder without the workshop lights. In the dark a small mistake seemed enormous; people were whispering, and someone had dropped a spoon that rang across the tiles like a bell.\n\n\"We've trained for power loss,\" Danse said under his breath.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why do I feel as though I'm improvising?\"\n\n\"Because this is happening to real people, not a training map.\"\n\nHe stared at the crate and helped Aster move it clear. The work was ordinary and unglamorous. He could have thrown the box aside without breaking a sweat, but he waited while Aster checked where it belonged. Renn was still monitoring the generator. Nobody needed him to command both places at once.\n\n\"Is this enough?\" he asked me.\n\n\"To make the path clear? Let's ask the person who uses it.\"\n\nWe did. The cart passed without catching the wall. Aster wrote the clearance in the log, and Danse looked less interested in being the first to claim the crisis was over.\n\nWhen he returned to the panel, Renn had new readings. He bent over the figures and listened.\n\nPip arrived with a sketch of the hall showing the two routes people were actually using. One led to the kitchens; the other went to the sheltered waiting rooms. The lamp beside the closed storage door had been placed there because the old plan showed it as an entrance.\n\n\"Could we move that one?\" I asked.\n\nPip nodded. \"The storage door's locked and signed. Nobody needs to go through it tonight.\"\n\nDanse considered.\n\n\"Provided the exit markings remain visible.\"\n\nThey walked the route together before moving anything. Aster carried the lamp, Renn checked the cable was clear of the footpath, and Pip tested whether the light could be seen from the hall's far corner.\n\nAn older traveler asked whether they could still leave by the eastern route.\n\n\"Yes,\" Pip said. \"It is open if you choose to use it. We can show you the map.\"\n\nThe traveler chose to wait until morning instead.\n\nNo one argued.\n\nBack at the yard, Danse wrote the temporary arrangement on the board and assigned a recheck before dawn.\n\n\"This system needs a permanent backup,\" he said.\n\n\"With a budget and a maintenance schedule,\" I added.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Naturally.\"\n\nI took his hand briefly.\n\nHe was still learning the hardest part: a good emergency plan did not depend on him staying awake forever. It depended on other people being able to follow it when he went home.\n\nThe backup power held until dawn. When the water level fell, the team completed the insulation test, replaced the damaged seal, and restarted the generator at reduced load. Lights returned to the workshop first, then the square.\n\nThere was no applause.\n\nThere was a list of repairs.\n\nWe posted the temporary load limit, the inspection results, and the next scheduled test. Renn added their signature beside the work they'd actually performed. Danse added his to the line documenting the missed conduit inspection.\n\nBy the time the last lantern was collected, my hands were shaking with exhaustion.\n\nDanse noticed.\n\n\"You're cold.\"\n\n\"I'm tired.\"\n\n\"Then come home.\"\n\n\"There's still work.\"\n\n\"There's always work.\"\n\nHe said it with such quiet conviction that I looked up.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"Renn and Aster have the next shift. Tamsin will review the repair. I've left the equipment in a safe state.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\nWe walked to his room above the workshop. The familiar smell of clean metal and dry wood met me as he opened the door. He set the toolbelt aside, took off his coat, and stood for a moment looking at the lamp on the table.\n\n\"I used to feel better only when the lights came back,\" he said.\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I feel better knowing nobody was hurt because I wanted to restore them too quickly.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"That matters.\"\n\nHe turned toward me.\n\n\"Do you want to stay?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Because you want to, not because it's late?\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Both can be true.\"\n\nHe laughed softly.\n\nHis kiss was careful at first, then warmer when I rested my hands against his chest. We stood close enough to hear the rain easing outside. No one needed a heroic performance from us.\n\nWhen he asked if I wanted to go inside, I said yes.\n\nWe closed the door and left the rest of the night private.\n\nIn the morning, there was a note from Renn.\n\nThe generator had passed its second test.\n\nUnderneath, in smaller writing, they had added: THANK YOU FOR NOT RUSHING.\n\nDanse read it twice.\n\nThen he folded it and put it in his pocket.","word_count":2003}};var s=lib.series.find(x=>x.series_slug==="what-the-armor-leaves-behind");if(!s)return;for(var i=0;i<s.chapters.length;i++){var c=s.chapters[i],e=edits[c.id];if(e)Object.assign(c,e);}s.total_word_count=83588;})();
